{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JNcFzqxYxovWdsiFNqHesB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Go implementations of each utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n\n- Several packages already have implementations in place: testutils with differential testing logic, sys with platform-specific file info handling, format with output utilities, and initial implementations of cat and du commands.\n\nLooking at the release status, there's a mismatch—all releases show code as not started, but actual implementations exist. The magefiles for release 00.0 are handled by the scaffold generator and excluded from the codebase, so the only real target there is the version command.\n\nFor releases 00.0 through 01.3, the code is already present: format utilities with color and column handling, system utilities with file info and platform support, test utilities, the cat command, the sponge command, and the du command all have working implementations with tests.\n\nStarting with release 02.0, the work shifts to pending specs with no code yet—the trivial utilities (true, false, yes, basename, dirname), tee, head, seq, and wc all need implementation. The ls core functionality is also spec-complete but waiting for code.\n\nFor release 00.0, the format, sys, and testutils modules already exist, but cmd/version hasn't been generated yet. I'm checking whether the magefiles spec covers this and what the actual PRD requirements are for the version command.\n\nLooking at the release timeline, I can see that releases 00.0 through 01.3 have code in place, though some are missing test files. Releases 02.0 and beyond are still pending specs, so I can't move forward on those until the requirements are defined.\n\nFor du in release 01.3, the main code exists but there's no test file visible, which seems like the most immediate gap to fill. And for wc in release 03.0, the spec is complete and there's apparently code already merged, though it's not showing in the current source view.\n\nGiven the constraint of picking just one task and following the priority rules around earliest incomplete releases and dependency ordering, release 00.0 with its version command is technically first, but release 01.3's missing du test file is the clearest actionable next step since the main implementation is already there. The metadata appears outdated since the code status shows items as \"not started\" but the source code clearly exists. For the du implementation in release 01.3, I have the main.go file but the test file is missing, making this the earliest incomplete item. Let me verify the PRD coverage by checking which requirements are actually implemented in the du code. R4.1, R4.2, R5.1\n\nLooking at the test suite specs — there should be a test-rel-01.3.yaml for du tests. But I don't have its content.\n\nFor the test file, I need to look at the pattern from existing tests (cat_test.go, sponge_test.go) and write differential tests for du.\n\nLet me look at the pattern:\n- cat_test.go: Tests R1.1, R1.2, R5.2, R5.3\n- sponge_test.go: Tests R1.1, R2.1, R3.1, R4.1\n\nFor du, the tests should verify the core requirements implemented in main.go.\n\nThe du reference binary would be `gdu` (GNU du via Homebrew). Following the same approach as cat_test.go, I'll set up TestMain to build the Go binary and locate the reference binary, then use individual test functions with testutils.RunDiffTests to verify specific requirements.\n\nGiven the constraint of at most 4 PRD sub-requirements per task, I need to focus on the most critical ones: R1.1 for recursive directory traversal, R1.2 for 1024-byte block units, and R1.3 for the SIZE\\tPATH output format. I'm settling on R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R1.5 as the four sub-requirements for the first test task, which will live in cmd/du/du_test.go. I need to reference the prd009-du PRD along with the existing du main.go, testutils package, and established test patterns as required reading. Before I finalize the task, I should verify whether I need to check the release 00.0 context first—though since the generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/, and I have the PRD anchor for du, I can proceed with writing the task based on prd009-du. requirements. I can anchor the test task to those.\n\nLet me also consider: should I propose a wc task instead? Release 03.0 has spec_complete for wc, and no code exists. But release 01.3 (du) is earlier and missing tests. By P1, I should focus on 01.3 first.\n\nOK, my decision: propose the du differential test task (cmd/du/du_test.go).\n\nLet me structure it:\n\nTitle: \"cmd/du: differential tests for prd009-du R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5\"\n\nRequired reading:\n- docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (du PRD)\n- cmd/du/main.go (existing implementation)\n- pkg/testutils/difftest.go (differential testing harness API)\n- cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference test pattern)\n\nFiles to create:\n- cmd/du/du_test.go — differential tests for du\n\nRequirements mapping four test cases to the PRD: recursive directory traversal reporting, 1024-byte block display units, SIZE\\tPATH output format, and default \".\" argument handling when none provided.\n\nDesign approach: TestMain will build the Go du binary and locate the gdu reference binary following the cat_test.go pattern, skip all tests if gdu isn't available, and use the testutils.RunDiffTests harness for all comparisons.\n\nKey implementation detail: create temporary directory structures with known file content to ensure deterministic block counts across both binaries.\n\nAcceptance criteria cover compilation, binary location, differential test passing, and coverage of the four main requirements.\n\nLooking at the du implementation more carefully, it's just basic du with no flags, so the tests should focus on that core behavior. Block counts should match between the Go and reference binaries when run on the same files, though they depend on the filesystem.\n\nFor the binary name, `gdu` is correct following the Homebrew pattern on macOS. Error messages will need normalization to handle \"gdu:\" vs \"du:\" prefixes, similar to how cat tests handle \"gcat:\".\n\nThe test file should land in the 100-200 line range based on similar test files, well under the 250 line limit. I need to create differential tests for the du command that verify recursive directory traversal, block unit conversion, and output formatting against a reference implementation, using the DiffTest framework from the existing test patterns. I need to normalize error messages by replacing \"gdu: \" with \"du: \" to match the reference binary's output format, and my test directory structures should have multiple nesting levels with both files and subdirectories to properly exercise the recursive traversal logic. The acceptance criteria cover compilation, binary building with graceful skipping when gdu isn't available, passing differential tests, and exercising the full feature set.\n\nWait, I'm reconsidering whether du_test.go is actually the right priority here. Looking at the release timeline, du has code but no tests yet, while cat and sponge are both complete with tests. That makes du_test.go the clear next task to tackle.\n\nBut I'm realizing I don't have the PRD content for the magefiles section, so I can't properly define requirements for cmd/version even though it's missing from the source code for release 00.0.\n\nThe du test file isn't included in the provided source code either, despite cat and sponge test files being there, which confirms du_test.go doesn't exist yet. That makes it the right anchor point since I can reference the existing du implementation and the PRD requirements.\n\nLooking at the block conversion logic, I see it transforms 512-byte blocks to 1024-byte blocks. The test should verify this matches the reference binary's behavior. I'm thinking the most testable requirements are R1.1 (recursion), R1.3 (format), R1.5 (default argument), and R4.2 (error handling), since the block unit conversion gets implicitly validated whenever I compare outputs against the reference—any mismatch there would show up immediately in the differential test.\n\nActually, that means all four core requirements (R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5) are effectively covered through differential comparison, so I'll stick with those. The constraint limits me to 4 PRD sub-requirements maximum, and these four represent the essential behavior I need to validate.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62034,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":62034},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dea49c0f-2c4a-4035-a67d-8c53d1a4fc89","uuid":"0205d595-ec97-4ae7-b0d8-d96621289fb8"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JNcFzqxYxovWdsiFNqHesB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded externally).\n\n**Current state from source code:**\n- **Code exists:** pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/cat (impl + tests), cmd/sponge (impl + tests), cmd/du (impl only — no test file in source_code)\n- **Spec complete, no code on branch:** cmd/wc, cmd/ls\n- **Specs pending:** true, false, yes, basename, dirname, tee, head, seq\n\n**Earliest incomplete release:** Release 01.3 (du) — implementation exists (`cmd/du/main.go`) but differential tests are missing. Releases 00.0, 01.1, 01.2 have code present. Release 01.3 is the first gap.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 01.3 (`rel01.3-uc001-du`) is the earliest release with incomplete work. `cmd/du/main.go` exists and implements prd009-du R1.1–R1.5, R4.1–R4.2, R5.1, but no `du_test.go` is present (unlike cat and sponge which both have test files). Per P5, tests follow implementation. This is the single highest-priority task.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/du: differential tests for prd009-du R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (du PRD -- all requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - cmd/du/main.go (existing du implementation covering R1.1-R1.5, R4.1-R4.2, R5.1)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests API)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for differential test file structure, TestMain, normalizeProgramName)\n      - cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go (reference pattern for test helpers and writeTestFile)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/du_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests for du core disk usage reporting\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test recursive directory traversal: create a temporary directory tree with files and nested subdirectories, run both gdu and Go du on the tree root, verify stdout matches byte-for-byte via testutils.RunDiffTests (prd009-du R1.1)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test 1024-byte block display units: verify that both binaries report identical block counts for the same directory tree, confirming the Go implementation's 512-to-1024 block conversion matches the reference binary (prd009-du R1.2)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test SIZE\\\\tPATH output format: verify that each output line produced by the Go binary matches the reference binary's format -- integer block count, tab character, path string -- for both directories and non-directory file arguments (prd009-du R1.3)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test default argument: when no arguments are given, du defaults to \\\".\\\"; run both binaries with no arguments inside a temporary directory and verify identical output (prd009-du R1.5)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"TestMain builds the Go du binary into a temporary directory via go build and locates the GNU reference binary via exec.LookPath(\\\"gdu\\\"). If gdu is not on PATH, print a skip message to stderr and exit 0 (matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go lines 29-34).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use testutils.RunDiffTests for all differential test cases. Each test function creates a temporary directory tree with known file content and passes DiffTest structs specifying Args, WorkDir, and ExitCode.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Include a normalizeProgramName function that replaces \\\"gdu: \\\" with \\\"du: \\\" in output bytes, matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go lines 66-68, so stderr from the GNU reference binary and the Go binary can be compared.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Test directory trees must contain at least two levels of nesting (parent containing a subdirectory containing files) to exercise the recursive walkDir path, and must include both directory and non-directory arguments to exercise both branches of duArg.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/du/du_test.go compiles without errors: go vet ./cmd/du/ exits 0\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"TestMain builds the Go du binary and locates gdu; tests are skipped cleanly with a message to stderr when gdu is not installed\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"All differential tests pass when gdu is available on PATH: go test ./cmd/du/ exits 0\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Tests cover recursive traversal (R1.1), block unit display (R1.2), output format (R1.3), and default argument (R1.5) through at least four distinct DiffTest cases\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Test file follows the project conventions: copyright header, SPDX license, package main, PRD requirement references in comments, and t.Parallel() on all test functions (matching cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go)\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62034,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":62034},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dea49c0f-2c4a-4035-a67d-8c53d1a4fc89","uuid":"2a74cefb-3d17-40db-86b9-fa8273ac1d4a"}
